<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ryan Alweiss
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics and Trinity College, University of Cambridge.
Email: <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ra699@cam.ac.uk</span>.
Research supported by an NSF Mathematical Sciences Postdoctoral Fellowship.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that there is a set which is not a set of multiple recurrence despite being a set of recurrence for nil-Bohr sets. This answers Huang, Shao, and Ye’s <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“higher-order”</span> version of Katznelson’s Question on Bohr recurrence and topological recurrence in the negative. Equivalently, we construct a set <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> so that there is a finite coloring of <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> without three-term arithmetic progressions with common differences in <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, but so that <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> lacks the usual polynomial obstacles to arithmetic progressions.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central and long-standing open problem in the field of topological dynamics is <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Katznelson’s Question”</span>, on whether a set of Bohr recurrence is a set of topological recurrence. This question was raised by a 1968 paper of Veech <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and implicitly before that in a 1954 paper of Følner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. However, in a classic example of Stigler’s law, it is often referred to as <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Katznelson’s question”</span>, because Katznelson popularized the question and wrote a beautiful paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> connecting it to the chromatic number of abelian Cayley graphs. In this paper, via a Furstenberg correspondence principle type argument in symbolic dynamics, he shows that Bohr recurrence is equivalent to topological recurrence if and only if every abelian Cayley graph with finite chromatic number has a finite coloring via Bohr sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us recall the equivalent dynamical and combinatorial definitions of Bohr and topological recurrence, and some basics of topological dynamics. These appear widely in the literature (see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>). Recall that <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||x||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}=\min(\{x\},1-\{x\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||x||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}=\min(\{x\},1-\{x\})</annotation></semantics></math> is the distance between <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and the nearest integer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bohr recurrence, combinatorial definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of Bohr recurrence if and only if for all nonzero <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{k}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{k}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists some <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> so that for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\alpha_{i}s||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\alpha_{i}s||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will also formally define a Bohr set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bohr set)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Bohr set is a set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by a <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of reals <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\alpha_{i}s||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\alpha_{i}s||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The parameter <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dimension</em> and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">width</em>. Later we will define a complexity of a nil-Bohr set, which will be <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(\max(k,1/\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(\max(k,1/\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math> for ordinary Bohr sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently a set is a set of Bohr recurrence if it is not contained in the complement of a Bohr set, or equivalently in a finite union of complements of one-dimensional Bohr sets. Sometimes this is referred to as a <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Bohr_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Bohr_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> set”</span> in the literature, with the term Bohr set referring to the more general class of translates of <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Bohr_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Bohr_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> sets. Here we will only deal with <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Bohr_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Bohr_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> sets, so we will refer to them simply as Bohr sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bohr recurrence, dynamical definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of Bohr recurrence if and only if for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and all rotations <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(x_{1},\cdots,x_{k})=(x_{1}+\alpha_{1},\cdots,x_{k}+\alpha_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(x_{1},\cdots,x_{k})=(x_{1}+\alpha_{1},\cdots,x_{k}+\alpha_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> on the torus <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a point <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and a subsequence <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in S</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{n}(x)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{n}(x)=x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The two definitions are readily seen to be equivalent. Since torus rotations are isometries, if <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{n}x\to x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{n}x\to x</annotation></semantics></math> is true for a single point then it is true for all points, so it would be equivalent to change the quantifier on <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> from <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“there exists”</span> to <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“for all”</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We only consider <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin S</annotation></semantics></math>, as if <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in S</annotation></semantics></math> then all notions of recurrence are trivial. In general dealing with <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to dealing with <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cup-S\subset\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0em">∪</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cup-S\subset\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we will assume for the remainder of this paper that <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is important, because a general topological system is not necessarily invertible, so <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> may not make sense.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that if <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of a group <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the Cayley graph <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Cay(G,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Cay(G,S)</annotation></semantics></math> on an abelian group <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has all elements of <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as vertices and <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent if and only if <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-b\in(S\cup-S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0em">∪</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-b\in(S\cup-S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall also that the chromatic number of a graph is the minimal number of colors needed to color the vertices so that no two vertices of the same color are adjacent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Topological recurrence, combinatorial definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of topological recurrence if and only if the Cayley graph <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Cay(\mathbb{Z},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Cay(\mathbb{Z},S)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has infinite chromatic number.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Topological recurrence is the most general form of recurrence, applying to all dynamical systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Topological recurrence, dynamical definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of topological recurrence if for every compact dynamical system <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,T)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact metric space and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous map on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a point <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> and a subsequence <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{n}x=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{n}x=x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">It was Katznelson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> who proved the equivalence of these two definitions of topological recurrence, giving the eponymous question its combinatorial form. One direction is relatively simple. If a set <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is not a set of dynamical topological recurrence, then one can use the dynamical system to come up with a coloring of the Cayley graph. In particular there will be some <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(T^{n}x,x)&gt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(T^{n}x,x)&gt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. Now cover the compact space by <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-balls for a suitably small <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and color <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> by which <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-ball <math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{m}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{m}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> ends up in. The other direction, using a coloring to construct a dynamical system, follows from an argument of Katznelson that makes use of symbolic dynamics, and bears some resemblance to the Furstenberg correspondence principle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">A set of topological recurrence must be a set of Bohr recurrence. This is readily seen both from the topological definition, as a circle rotation is a special kind of dynamical system, and from the combinatorial version, as if <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> a set that is not a set of Bohr recurrence it is easy to construct a finite coloring by taking all of the <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, up to an error of <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we can state Katznelson’s question explicitly as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Katznelson’s question)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of Bohr recurrence, then is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> necessarily a set of topological recurrence?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> it is stated that there is <span id="S1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“no consensus among experts as to the expected answer”</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">No discussion of the history of Katznelson’s question would be complete without also referencing the <span id="S1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Related Problem”</span>. Bergelson asked whether there is a set which is a set of topological recurrence but not a set of measurable recurrence, or equivalently whether Cayley graph on the integers with a positive upper density independent set, but infinite chromatic number, and Kříž <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> resolved this question in the negative. His construction is based on the boolean Cayley graph over <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by elements of Hamming weight <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;n-10\sqrt{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;n-10\sqrt{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can check that elements of Hamming weight <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq n/2-10\sqrt{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq n/2-10\sqrt{n}</annotation></semantics></math> form a positive density independent set in this graph. Furthermore, looking at the elements of Hamming weight <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/2-6\sqrt{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/2-6\sqrt{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, this graph has a copy of the Kneser graph <math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KG(n,n/2-6\sqrt{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KG(n,n/2-6\sqrt{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and thus has chromatic number at least <math id="S1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and goes to infinity with <math id="S1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Kříž projects this construction to the integers. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> for an exposition of his example by Griesmer and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> for an extension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we will be concerned with the natural higher-order version of Katznelson’s question. This was first explicitly asked by Huang, Shao, and Ye <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, is the main subject of their book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and was asked again as Question 7 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Before stating it, we need to define topological multiple recurrence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Topological multiple recurrence, dynamical definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of topological <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-recurrence if for every compact dynamical system <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,T)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact metric space and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous map on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a point <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> and a subsequence <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> so that for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq d^{\prime}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq d^{\prime}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{d^{\prime}n}x=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{d^{\prime}n}x=x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{n}x,T^{2n}x,\cdots,T^{dn}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{n}x,T^{2n}x,\cdots,T^{dn}x</annotation></semantics></math> all go to <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> along this sequence <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. So we have <math id="S1.p15.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T^{n}x\to x,T^{2n}x\to x,\cdots,T^{dn}x\to x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{n}x\to x,T^{2n}x\to x,\cdots,T^{dn}x\to x</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously along this sequence <math id="S1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math>, this is the ordinary notion of recurrence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Topological multiple recurrence, combinatorial definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of topological <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-recurrence if for every finite coloring of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a monochromatic <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression with common difference in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, the <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-hypergraph with edges <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,x+s,\cdots,x+ds)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,x+s,\cdots,x+ds)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> has infinite chromatic number. In other words, restricted van der Waerden with differences in <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is true for <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progressions. For <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math> this is readily seen to coincide with the Cayley graph definition. As before, this combinatorial definition is equivalent to the dynamical one, see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, Theorem 2.5. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, the notation <math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{Bir_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{Bir_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used for the family of sets of topological <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-recurrence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">For restricted van der Waerden, on top of the usual obstacles in Katznelson’s question, there are higher degree obstacles. If <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is far from a multiple of an irrational number like <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x+s,x+2s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x+s,x+2s</annotation></semantics></math> cannot all be too close modulo <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2s^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is far from a multiple of <math id="S1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, then because <math id="S1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}-2(x+s)^{2}+(x+2s)^{2}=2s^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}-2(x+s)^{2}+(x+2s)^{2}=2s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the squares of <math id="S1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x+s,x+2s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x+s,x+2s</annotation></semantics></math> cannot all be very close to each other modulo <math id="S1.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> as well, which yields a finite coloring where <math id="S1.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is colored by <math id="S1.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S1.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> up to a small error. In general there will be polynomial obstacles up to degree <math id="S1.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. So the natural question here is whether there are any other obstacles, with the caveat that one has to also worry about <span id="S1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“generalized polynomials”</span> or <span id="S1.p17.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“bracket polynomials”</span> like <math id="S1.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\lfloor s\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\lfloor s\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. These generalized polynomials are formally defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, but we will not define them yet.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">In the dynamics literature, these correspond to nilmanifolds. Multiple recurrence for Bohr sets is equivalent to single recurrence, but there is a higher-order notion of Bohr recurrence called <span id="S1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“nil-Bohr recurrence”</span>. It was originally defined dynamically in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and is the main object of study in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. We state it here for completeness. For a formal definition of a (<math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-step) nilsystem, one can for instance consult <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, but again it is not so crucial for the present paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.9</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(nil-Bohr multiple recurrence, dynamical definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence if for every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-step nil-system <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,T)</annotation></semantics></math> there is a point <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> and a subsequence <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> so that for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq d^{\prime}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq d^{\prime}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{d^{\prime}n}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\lim_{n\in S^{\prime}}T^{d^{\prime}n}x</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">As usual, there is also a combinatorial definition. Again, we state it here for completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.10</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(nil-Bohr multiple recurrence, combinatorial definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence if for every finite family of generalized polynomials <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\cdots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\cdots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with degree at most <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||P_{i}(s)||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||P_{i}(s)||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">The set of <math id="S1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> so that these generalized polynomials have small <math id="S1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Nil-Bohr”</span> set, or a <span id="S1.p20.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“<math id="S1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr”</span> set when we want to emphasize the parameter of <math id="S1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. An ordinary Bohr set is just a <math id="S1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">The equivalence of the definitions is Theorem B of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, where the notation <math id="S1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{d,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{d,0}</annotation></semantics></math> is used for the family of <math id="S1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr sets and <math id="S1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{d,0}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{d,0}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for the family of sets of <math id="S1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence. Again, we will not formally define <span id="S1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“generalized polynomial”</span>, but it is essentially just a polynomial with floors and ceilings, often also referred to as a <span id="S1.p21.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“bracket polynomial”</span> in the literature (see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>). The <span id="S1.p21.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“degree”</span> of a generalized polynomial is just the degree in <math id="S1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> ignoring the floors and ceilings, so for instance <math id="S1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\lfloor s\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\lfloor s\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> will have degree <math id="S1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Huang, Shao, and Ye also prove that one can also equivalently ask for the generalized polynomials <math id="S1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <span id="S1.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“special”</span> generalized polynomials of an even simpler form. We will define these. First, following Section 4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> we use the notation <math id="S1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x]:=\lfloor x+1/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x]:=\lfloor x+1/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> for the nearest integer function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.11</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Special Generalized Polynomials, after <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(x)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(x)=x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(x_{1},\dots,x_{\ell})=x_{1}[L(x_{2},\dots,x_{\ell})]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(x_{1},\dots,x_{\ell})=x_{1}[L(x_{2},\dots,x_{\ell})].</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then the special generalized polynomials of degree at most <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> are the
expressions</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(n^{j_{1}}a_{1},\dots,n^{j_{\ell}}a_{\ell})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(n^{j_{1}}a_{1},\dots,n^{j_{\ell}}a_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p2.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}j_{i}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}j_{i}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p23.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we must have <math id="S1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.12</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(nil-Bohr multiple recurrence, simple combinatorial definition)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence if for every finite family of special generalized polynomials <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\cdots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\cdots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with degree at most <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||P_{i}(s)||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||P_{i}(s)||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p24.1" class="ltx_p">We have for instance that the set of <math id="S1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/4&lt;\{n^{2}\alpha\}&lt;3/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/4&lt;\{n^{2}\alpha\}&lt;3/4</annotation></semantics></math> and the set of <math id="S1.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S1.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/4&lt;\{\alpha n[n]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/4&lt;\{\alpha n[n]\}</annotation></semantics></math> are not sets of <math id="S1.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence, even though they are sets of Bohr recurrence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p25.1" class="ltx_p">The equivalence of the simple and ordinary combinatorial definitions of nil-Bohr multiple recurrence is Theorem 4.2.11 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In our proof, we will instead use a somewhat different form of <span id="S1.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“special generalized polynomial”</span> from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Leibman proves in his paper that these have the property that they jointly equi-distribute.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p26.1" class="ltx_p">The natural higher-order analogue of Katznelson’s question is whether topological multiple recurrence is equivalent to nil-Bohr multiple recurrence, and specifically whether topological <math id="S1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-recurrence is equivalent to <math id="S1.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence. This is Question B, the main question asked in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, which in formulation B-III asks whether <math id="S1.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{Bir_{d}}=\mathcal{F}^{*}_{d,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{Bir_{d}}=\mathcal{F}^{*}_{d,0}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we answer this question in the negative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.13</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> which is not a set of topological <math id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-recurrence, but is a set of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p27.1" class="ltx_p">We really only need that it is a set of <math id="S1.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence, but it turns out to be no more difficult to also get this for <math id="S1.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. The same proof works.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.14</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a set <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> which is not a set of topological <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-recurrence, but is a set of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr recurrence for every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, we find a set <math id="S1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> so that restricted van der Waerden with common difference <math id="S1.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> is false even for three-term progressions, but <math id="S1.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> intersects all <math id="S1.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Nil_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Nil_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bohr sets, and so it does not have quadratic obstacles like <math id="S1.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s:||s^{2}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&gt;\varepsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s:||s^{2}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&gt;\varepsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math> (or higher degree obstacles like <math id="S1.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s:||s^{3}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&gt;\varepsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s:||s^{3}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&gt;\varepsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math>) that would give an <span id="S1.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“obvious obstruction”</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem14" title="Theorem 1.14. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.14</span></a></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To prove <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem14" title="Theorem 1.14. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.14</span></a> we must ultimately construct a set <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we will first construct a set <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in a different setting, and consider an appropriate projection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of sequences <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},\cdots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math> of elements of <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is less than <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. This is an abelian group endowed with the usual addition operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The key fact about this group <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> that we will exploit is that it is not a Hilbert space and so has no true <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“quadratic structure”</span> (and similarly, no true cubic structure or quartic structure and so on). While one can define the square of the <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm to be <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||^{2}_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||^{2}_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> which will be bounded by the usual <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}||a_{i}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> (and similarly one can define other <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> norms), one can check that the parallelogram law <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||x+d||^{2}_{\ell^{2}}-2||x||^{2}_{\ell_{2}}+||x-d||_{\ell_{2}}^{2}=2||d||^{2}_{\ell_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||x+d||^{2}_{\ell^{2}}-2||x||^{2}_{\ell_{2}}+||x-d||_{\ell_{2}}^{2}=2||d||^{2}_{\ell_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not hold. For instance one can set <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(0,1/3,2/3,0,\cdots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(0,1/3,2/3,0,\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=(1/3,1/3,1/3,0,\cdots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=(1/3,1/3,1/3,0,\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||d||^{2}_{2}=1/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||d||^{2}_{2}=1/3</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||x-d||_{2}=||x||_{2}=||x+d||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||x-d||_{2}=||x||_{2}=||x+d||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“fake quadratic structure”</span> from this <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm and from the curvature of the circle will enable us to come up with a coloring that prevents three-term monochromatic progressions. Over the integers, the <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm will end up corresponding to something like an infinite sum of squares of fractional parts <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\{na_{i}\}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\{na_{i}\}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which is an infinite-dimensional generalized polynomial and does not simplify to a low-dimensional generalized polynomial like <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{n^{2}\alpha\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{n^{2}\alpha\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\sum a_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\sum a_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now describe the construction.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Building the <math id="S2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> example</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be small positive numbers so that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}/\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}/\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a suitably small function of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so for instance <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}&lt;\delta_{1}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}&lt;\delta_{1}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example one can pick <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}=10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}=10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}=10^{-10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}=10^{-10}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, for ease of notation we will write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset of sequences <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(a_{1},a_{2},\cdots,)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},a_{2},\cdots,)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> so that the following hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is some <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in(-\delta_{1}-2\delta_{2},\,-\delta_{1}+2\delta_{2})\subset\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in(-\delta_{1}-2\delta_{2},\,-\delta_{1}+2\delta_{2})\subset\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}\in(-2\delta_{2},2\delta_{2})\subset\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}\in(-2\delta_{2},2\delta_{2})\subset\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}-2\delta_{2}&lt;\sum_{j\neq i}||a_{j}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\delta_{1}+2\delta_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}-2\delta_{2}&lt;\sum_{j\neq i}||a_{j}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\delta_{1}+2\delta_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, some <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is in a small interval around <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and the other <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are all very close to an integer so that their <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> norms add up to about <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also define a finitary version <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> supported only on the first <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates. We use the notation <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> for the space <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with the usual norm <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||(a_{1},\cdots,a_{m})||=\sum_{i=1}^{m}||a_{i}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||(a_{1},\cdots,a_{m})||=\sum_{i=1}^{m}||a_{i}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will need this later for compactness purposes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{m}=2^{-100m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{m}=2^{-100m}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset of sequences <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},a_{2},\cdots,a_{m})\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},a_{2},\cdots,a_{m})\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> so that the following hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is some <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in(-\delta_{1}-(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2},\,-\delta_{1}+(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2})\subset\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in(-\delta_{1}-(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2},\,-\delta_{1}+(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2})\subset\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}\in(-(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2},(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2})\subset\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}\in(-(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2},(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2})\subset\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}-(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2}&lt;\sum_{j\neq i}||a_{j}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\delta_{1}+(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}-(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2}&lt;\sum_{j\neq i}||a_{j}||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\delta_{1}+(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that this the same definition except with the interval bounds <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2-\eta_{m})\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for some small constants <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{m}=2^{-100m}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{m}=2^{-100m}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We must consider such <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with positive and decreasing <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> for compactness purposes. We may set <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\infty}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\infty}=0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\infty}=S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\infty}=S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We first check that <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> meets every Bohr set in this setting. This will be important later, when we project <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> down to <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Also for <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> we define <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(x)|</annotation></semantics></math> to be the maximum of the <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> norms of the coordinates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bohr set, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{N}\cup\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{N}\cup\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is a Bohr set if there exists <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and a linear function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})\to(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})\to(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(b)|&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(b)|&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, we must check that for all linear functions <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})\to(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})\to(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are points of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that are mapped arbitrarily close to the origin. What we actually need is a finitary version of this.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be a Bohr set with dimension <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> so that if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{N}\cup\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{N}\cup\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;g(k,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;g(k,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}\cap B\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}\cap B\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}e_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}e_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the vector that is <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th coordinate and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in all other coordinates. For all <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large as a function of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> we can find a set <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\delta_{1}/\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\delta_{1}/\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> natural numbers so that the following property holds. Call one of these numbers <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for any of the other numbers <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the vector <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}:=f(\delta_{2}e_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}:=f(\delta_{2}e_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is within <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of the vector <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:=f(\delta_{2}e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:=f(\delta_{2}e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f_{j}-f_{i}|\leq\delta_{2}\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f_{j}-f_{i}|\leq\delta_{2}\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where the norm is taken in <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The vector <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with coordinate <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th coordinate, <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th coordinate for <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\ni j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\ni j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in all other coordinates is in <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> by definition. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{T\ni j\neq i}(\delta_{2}e_{j}-\delta_{2}e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{T\ni j\neq i}(\delta_{2}e_{j}-\delta_{2}e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, by the triangle inequality the norm of <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(v)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{T\ni j\neq i}||f_{j}-f_{i}||\leq(\delta_{1}/\delta_{2})\delta_{2}\varepsilon=\delta_{1}\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{T\ni j\neq i}||f_{j}-f_{i}||\leq(\delta_{1}/\delta_{2})\delta_{2}\varepsilon=\delta_{1}\varepsilon&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. The same argument shows that <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> meets a Bohr set as long as <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is a large enough function of the dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and the inverse-width <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of the Bohr set. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In fact we only used here that the function is additive, since <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}/\delta_{2}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}/\delta_{2}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The astute reader may notice that the linear functions <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})\to\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})\to\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> comprise the dual group <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\infty}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\infty}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, are given by a sequence of integers <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b_{1},b_{2},\cdots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b_{1},b_{2},\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math> with some <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|b_{i}|\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|b_{i}|\leq C</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and send <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},a_{2},\cdots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},a_{2},\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\in\mathbb{N}}a_{i}b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\in\mathbb{N}}a_{i}b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, a function to <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> will consist of <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such functions, one for each coordinate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>The coloring</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe the coloring on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(x)=e^{2\pi ix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(x)=e^{2\pi ix}</annotation></semantics></math> be the exponential function from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> to the unit circle in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note by the mean value theorem that <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|e(x)-1|\leq 2\pi||x||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|e(x)-1|\leq 2\pi||x||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> where the norm on the left-hand side is taken in the complex numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will color the sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(a_{1},\cdots,a_{m})\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(a_{1},\cdots,a_{m})\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> by the value of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\left(e(a_{i})-1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\left(e(a_{i})-1\right)</annotation></semantics></math> or in <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, taken mod the Gaussian integers <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[i]</annotation></semantics></math> up to a small error. Since <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(x)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(x)-1</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by a constant multiple of the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> norm, for any <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> will be well-defined even when <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Partition <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{Z}[i]\cong(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{Z}[i]\cong(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\delta^{2}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\delta^{2}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> sets of diameter at most <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Color <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},\cdots,a_{m})\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},\cdots,a_{m})\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> by the set that contains <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{m}(e(a_{i})-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{m}(e(a_{i})-1)</annotation></semantics></math> taken modulo <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that our coloring lacks three-term monochromatic progressions with common difference in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,s\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,s\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N}_{\leq m};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is colored as in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.2 The coloring ‣ 2 Proof of ‣ New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+s</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+2s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+2s</annotation></semantics></math> will not all receive the same color.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Before proving this, we recall a basic fact from geometry and trigonometry. If we consider three equally spaced points on the unit circle, if they cut out an arc which is about a <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> proportion of the circle, then the middle point will have distance about <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> from the midpoint of the arc, by the standard Taylor approximation <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\cos(\delta)=\delta^{2}/2+O(\delta^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\cos(\delta)=\delta^{2}/2+O(\delta^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. This distance is called the <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“sagitta”</span>. So the contribution to <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s)</annotation></semantics></math> from the big <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> term so that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\approx\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\approx\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> will be about <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the other contributions from the <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> terms will in total at most <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\delta_{1}}{\delta_{2}}\delta^{2}_{2}\approx\delta_{1}\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\delta_{1}}{\delta_{2}}\delta^{2}_{2}\approx\delta_{1}\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which is smaller.</p>
</div>
