<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(s_{1},\cdots,s_{n})\in S\subset\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(s_{1},\cdots,s_{n})\in S\subset\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> and pick <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a sufficiently small constant so that <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer, and let <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}=\delta_{1}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}=\delta_{1}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> be an even smaller constant. In a slight abuse notation, we will use <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> for quantities that stay constant as <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, but we will end up making <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> a small enough constant itself. For any <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\cdots,x_{n},\cdots)\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\cdots,x_{n},\cdots)\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that:</span></p>
