<p id="S2.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">So if we choose <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a small enough constant, <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(\delta^{2}_{1})&gt;2\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(\delta^{2}_{1})&gt;2\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is less than <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/100</annotation></semantics></math>, and is far greater than <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as well. This means that <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x),f(x+s),f(x+2s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x),f(x+s),f(x+2s)</annotation></semantics></math> cannot all be within distance <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of each other in <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{Z}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{Z}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, as if they were then <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s)=(f(x)-f(x+s))-(f(x+s)-f(x+2s))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)-2f(x+s)+f(x+2s)=(f(x)-f(x+s))-(f(x+s)-f(x+2s))</annotation></semantics></math> would be within <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of a Gaussian integer by the triangle inequality. Hence <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x+s,x+2s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x+s,x+2s</annotation></semantics></math> cannot all receive the same color under our coloring, and there is no monochromatic <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-term arithmetic progression with difference <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Projecting to <math id="S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now need to project the example <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> from the previous subsection onto a set <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S_{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ℕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S_{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that in order to avoid a notational clash for <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, we refer to the sets from the previous section as <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> and we refer to the image of the projection of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ℕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will pick a sufficiently quickly shrinking sequence of rationally independent irrational numbers <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\cdots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the projection map <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> sending <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to(\{\alpha_{1}n\},\{\alpha_{2}n\},\cdots)\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to(\{\alpha_{1}n\},\{\alpha_{2}n\},\cdots)\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. We will pick our set of integers <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S_{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ℕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S_{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the pre-image of the set <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\infty}\subset\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\infty}\subset\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> from the last section under this map. Since <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a homomorphism, it is immediate to verify that there is a finite coloring of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> so that there are no monochromatic <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-APs in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> with common difference in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(S)=S_{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ℕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(S)=S_{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Simply color <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> by the color of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(n)\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(n)\in\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. We also mention here that since <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> shrink sufficiently quickly, it is immediate that the image will be dense in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}(\mathbb{N};\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> with the usual <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It remains to also check that <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ℕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects any finite number of Bohr sets and nil-Bohr sets. We dealt with the Bohr sets in the last section, and to show that higher-order nil-Bohr sets are not an issue we will use standard results about the independence of higher degree generalized polynomials up to easily handled obstructions. Since <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed only from linear Bohr sets <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is going to generally be independent of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n^{2}\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n^{2}\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and intersect the set of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||n^{2}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||n^{2}\alpha||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Of course <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n^{2}\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n^{2}\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{(n^{2}+n)\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{(n^{2}+n)\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> will not be linearly independent of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so we may get constantly many more linear Bohr obstructions that can arise in this way.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fortunately generalized polynomials can be generated by <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“basic generalized polynomials”</span> that do equi-distribute unless there is a linear obstruction, so the usual Bohr set obstacles are all we need to check. In particular by the main result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> we may write our generalized polynomials as piecewise polynomial functions of these <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“basic generalized polynomials”</span> and so any nil-Bohr set contains a nil-Bohr set generated by these basic generalized polynomials. We remark also that the <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“basic generalized polynomials”</span> of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> are slightly different than the <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“special generalized polynomials”</span> of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and that Leibman uses the abbreviation <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“gen-polynomials”</span> for generalized polynomials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now proceed to a rigorous proof. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of positive numbers that goes to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently quickly, and pick <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> going to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently quickly depending on <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ℕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the inverse image of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> under the projection <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be a nil-Bohr set. By Theorem 0.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> contains a nil-Bohr set generated by basic generalized polynomials <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and by Theorem 0.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> they jointly equi-distribute. Pick <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=m(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=m(B)</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large depending on <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the map <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{m},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{m},B)</annotation></semantics></math> sending <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\cdots\{n\alpha_{m}\},B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\cdots\{n\alpha_{m}\},B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=k(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=k(B)</annotation></semantics></math>. So we define <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{m}(n)=(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\cdots\{n\alpha_{m}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{m}(n)=(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\cdots\{n\alpha_{m}\})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(n)=(B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(n)=(B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">subtorus</em> of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be the set of points where some set of linear forms with integer coefficients vanish, and the codimension is the number of independent linear forms needed to generate this vanishing set. For our purposes we need a slight modification of Leibman’s result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Leibman)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of equi-distributed basic generalized polynomials and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are rationally independent irrational numbers, then the image of the map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\{n\alpha_{m}\},B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\{n\alpha_{m}\},B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> which we denote as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{m}(n),B(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{m}(n),B(n))</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-dense in some <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{k}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> codimension sub-torus of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem 0.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, the last <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates will equidistribute over the torus <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now reveal the <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> one by one. At each step, the new <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> will either be rationally independent from all of our existing gen-polynomials <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{n\beta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{n\beta\}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. the already revealed <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{n\alpha_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{n\alpha_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{n\beta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{n\beta\}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>), in which case <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{n\alpha_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{n\alpha_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> will jointly equidistribute, or it will be a rational linear combination of the <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are rationally independent, the latter case will occur only <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{k}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> times. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7 (Leibman). ‣ 2.3 Projecting to ℤ ‣ 2 Proof of ‣ New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, it is equivalent to restrict <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to be bounded by some function <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. For our purposes we will need an infinitary corollary of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7 (Leibman). ‣ 2.3 Projecting to ℤ ‣ 2 Proof of ‣ New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>. We first record a slightly refined version.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">First there exists a <span id="S2.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“complexity”</span> function <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> on nil-Bohr neighborhoods with some useful properties. We say that <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> has complexity at most <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> if it can be expressed as the pointwise limit of nil-Bohr neighborhoods with dimension at most <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, degree at most <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and width at least <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/C</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a bound on the parameters of the nil-Bohr set. In order to compute the exact complexity of <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the infimum of these upper bounds. In particular, this complexity function goes to infinity with the inverse width <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, the degree <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and the dimension <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and a pointwise limit of <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> with complexity at most <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> also has complexity at most <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Leibman, refined)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an explicit <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> so that for any nil-Bohr set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F((\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m}),\varepsilon,B)\leq F^{\prime}((\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m}),c(B,\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F((\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m}),\varepsilon,B)\leq F^{\prime}((\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m}),c(B,\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove this via compactness. Assume there exists <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> and a sequence of <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(B,\varepsilon)\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(B,\varepsilon)\leq C</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F((\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m}),\varepsilon,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F((\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m}),\varepsilon,B)</annotation></semantics></math> tends to infinity. By compactness, a subsequence of the <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> has some nil-Bohr set <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> also of complexity bounded by <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> as a pointwise limit. But then <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m},B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell})=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m},B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell})=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We need an infinitary corollary of this refinement for our purposes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Leibman, infinitary corollary)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a sufficiently quickly shrinking sequence <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the following holds. Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\cdots,B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be equi-distributed basic generalized polynomials. If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=m(\varepsilon,B_{1},\cdots,B_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=m(\varepsilon,B_{1},\cdots,B_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, then the image of the map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\mapsto(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\{n\alpha_{m}\},B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n))=(P_{m}(n),B(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\mapsto(\{n\alpha_{1}\},\cdots,\{n\alpha_{m}\},B_{1}(n),\cdots,B_{k}(n))=(P_{m}(n),B(n))</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> ranging and sufficiently smaller than <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\alpha_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\alpha_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-dense in some <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{k}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> codimension sub-torus of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 2.8 (Leibman, refined). ‣ 2.3 Projecting to ℤ ‣ 2 Proof of ‣ New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> we may pick a sequence of <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that goes to zero quickly enough so that for any <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, for sufficiently large <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F(\alpha_{1},\cdots\alpha_{m},B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\alpha_{1},\cdots\alpha_{m},B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently smaller than <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\alpha_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\alpha_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance if we guarantee that <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\alpha_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\alpha_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is much larger than <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{\prime}(\alpha_{1},\cdots\alpha_{m},m)\geq F(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m},B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}(\alpha_{1},\cdots\alpha_{m},m)\geq F(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{m},B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(B,\varepsilon)\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(B,\varepsilon)\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any fixed <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> we will have that <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F(\alpha_{1},\cdots\alpha_{m},B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\alpha_{1},\cdots\alpha_{m},B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> will be sufficiently smaller than <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\alpha_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\alpha_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> which is a large enough function of <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=c(B,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=c(B,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Note that in the above theorem, if we remove the size bound, and do not require that <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> must be small compared to <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\alpha_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\alpha_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the image will be dense.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If we take an appropriate sequence of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the conditions of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 2.9 (Leibman, infinitary corollary). ‣ 2.3 Projecting to ℤ ‣ 2 Proof of ‣ New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>, then for any nil-Bohr set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(S)\cap B\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(S)\cap B\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence that goes to <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently quickly. Choose <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large so that <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> meets all ordinary Bohr sets of complexity <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{k}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then as <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> ranges, the image <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{m}(n),B(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{m}(n),B(n))</annotation></semantics></math> will intersect <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}\times(-\varepsilon_{k},\varepsilon_{k})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}\times(-\varepsilon_{k},\varepsilon_{k})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, because it will be dense in an <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{k}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>-codimension subtorus of <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m+k}</annotation></semantics></math> and because <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is open in <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. A priori there is no bound on such <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and it depends on <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> as well as the specific choice of <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{k}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>-complexity <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. However, by compactness of complexity <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{k}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> Nil-Bohr sets <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{\ell},\varepsilon_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>, we can find a uniform bound on <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and so we can find such an <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently smaller than <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\alpha_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\alpha_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, we may choose an appropriate sequence of <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> so that for any nil-Bohr set <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, for sufficiently large <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> depending on <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> ranges but stays sufficiently small compared to <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\alpha_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\alpha_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{m}(n),B(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{m}(n),B(n))</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}\times(-\varepsilon_{k},\varepsilon_{k})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}\times(-\varepsilon_{k},\varepsilon_{k})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> was chosen to be sufficiently large so that <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> meets all ordinary Bohr sets of complexity <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{k}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{k}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">So we can find some <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{m}(n)\in S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{m}(n)\in S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently smaller than <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\alpha_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\alpha_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the size bound on <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(n)\in S_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(n)\in S_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in S_{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ℕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in S_{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the coordinates of <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(n)</annotation></semantics></math> have <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> norm less than <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is in the nil-Bohr set <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. This concludes the proof. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">A remark about nilmanifolds is in order. We do not use them here, but our results could be phrased equivalently in those terms. Section 2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> describes the correspondence between equi-distribution of generalized polynomials and the equi-distribution over nilmanifolds from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. The correspondence between generalized polynomials and nilmanifolds was first proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and is cited as Lemma 2.3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Theorem 2.9 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> is the quantiative version of Theorem 1.9 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, the main theorem of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. As written, our construction is not explicit because of the compactness arguments. Using the quantitative results of Green-Tao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, one can remedy this and make all of our compactness arguments effective.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Concluding Remarks and Open Questions</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most obvious open question here is Katznelson’s question. In view of our disproof of the higher-order version, we conjecture that the answer should be negative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a set of Bohr recurrence but not a set of topological recurrence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As usual we also state the combinatorial form of this conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the Cayley graph on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has a finite chromatic number, but does not admit a finite coloring via Bohr sets.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One reason to believe Katznelson’s question has a positive answer is that a <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“zero density exception”</span> version is known due to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. In particular, if <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense subset of <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-S</annotation></semantics></math> contains a <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-o(1)</annotation></semantics></math> proportion of a Bohr set. Katznelson’s question is equivalent to the same claim except with <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> syndetic and without the <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o(1)</annotation></semantics></math> proportion, see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. A similar <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“zero density exception”</span> of the higher-order Katznelson was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, but we have proved here higher-order Katznelson has a negative answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We generally believe that if Katznelson’s question has a positive answer, the proof somehow relies on topology. Indeed Glasscock, Koutsogiannis, and Richter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> prove the special case of skew products over <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by using the intermediate value theorem, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> relies on the topological lower bound for the chromatic number of the Kneser graph. We also note here that the construction in this paper can be expressed as coming from a skew product over <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We mention one important obstacle to extending something like our construction to settle the full Katznelson’s question. Let <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be an Abelian group. Then an element of <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> in the generating set with sum of coordinates <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> can be written in the form <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1}-a_{2},a_{2}-a_{3},\cdots,a_{m-1}-a_{m},a_{m}-a_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1}-a_{2},a_{2}-a_{3},\cdots,a_{m-1}-a_{m},a_{m}-a_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, so the cyclic shifts <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},\cdots,a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},\cdots,a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{2},a_{3},\cdots,a_{n},a_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{2},a_{3},\cdots,a_{n},a_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, we cannot simply take a function of each coordinate and add it up the way we considered <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e(x)-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e(x)-1)</annotation></semantics></math> here. This kind of <span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“cyclic shift”</span> obstacle destroys many potential counterexamples to Katznelson’s question, and any kind of high-dimensional projection must break this sort of symmetry. Nonetheless, we believe that our construction represents substantial progress towards Katznelson’s Question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Another interesting direction is to consider the finite field setting. One can also consider analogues of Katznelson’s question over positive characteristic. This was asked by Ben Green over characteristic <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, Problems 52 and 53) and also by John Griesmer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> over more general characteristic <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. We can similarly ask the higher-order version of Katznelson’s question in characteristic <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;d</annotation></semantics></math>. We state two such versions for <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-term progressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},p_{2},\cdots,p_{n},\cdots\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},p_{2},\cdots,p_{n},\cdots\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> be an increasing sequence of primes, and say we have a sequence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of Cayley graphs on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with uniformly bounded chromatic number <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Is there a uniform constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> so that the generating set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the union of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||P_{j}(x)/p||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&gt;1/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||P_{j}(x)/p||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}&gt;1/K</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are polynomials on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, can we always find a bounded number <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of quadratics <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> so that any <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in the generating set is mapped by at least one of the quadratics to a point that is not in the interval <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(-p/K,p/K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-p/K,p/K)</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed prime, and say we have a sequence of Cayley graphs <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Z}/p)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Z}/p)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for a sequence of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with uniformly bounded chromatic number <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Is there a uniform constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> so that the generating set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the union of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}(x)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}(x)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are quadratic forms on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Z}/p)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Z}/p)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A few comments are in order about why our example does not immediately resolve the above questions. Our example in some sense relies on the infinite bracket polynomial <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}\{na_{i}\}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}\{na_{i}\}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In modulo <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent. However, in modulo <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> the value of <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> determines the value of <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}(na_{i}\mod p)^{2}=n^{2}(\sum a_{i}^{2})\mod p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}(na_{i}\mod p)^{2}=n^{2}(\sum a_{i}^{2})\mod p</annotation></semantics></math>. Our example also relies heavily on the existence of a norm, which is less straightforward in positive characteristic, though one can consider the <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-adic norm or <math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||x/p||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||x/p||_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In general, it is also true that <math id="S3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“look different”</span> from the standpoint of arithmetic progressions of length <math id="S3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> or greater. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> for discussion about this.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">One can also ask if any <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> which is not a set of nil-Bohr has some kind of <span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“quadratic structure”</span>. We generally suspect the answer is no, and in fact that we do not think it is possible to write down a <span id="S3.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“nice”</span> set of criteria that determines whether <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of multiple recurrence or if <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of single recurrence. There is likely some logical or computability theoretic separation between nil-Bohr recurrence and multiple recurrence, as well as between Bohr recurrence and topological recurrence. We state a complexity theoretic conjecture in the finitary setting, which implies that the answer to Katznelson’s Question is negative in a strong sense. One could formulate a similar finitary conjecture for the higher-order version.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an absolute constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> so that for every constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, it is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NP</annotation></semantics></math>-hard to distinguish if an Abelian Cayley Graph that has chromatic number <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq c</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For an Abelian Cayley graph, we can determine in polynomial time whether there exists a coloring by Bohr sets using <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;C</annotation></semantics></math> colors by simply checking all such colorings, and in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> it is proved that Katznelson’s Question is equivalent to the statement that the number of colors necessary in such a coloring is bounded a function of the chromatic number. Hence, if Katznelson’s Question has a positive answer, there is a polynomial algorithm for this <span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Approximate Graph Coloring”</span> question for Abelian Cayley graphs. For general graphs, such problems are known to be NP-hard conditional on a variant of the Unique Games Conjecture, which gives us reason to believe <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Conjecture 3.5. ‣ 3 Concluding Remarks and Open Questions ‣ New Obstacles to Multiple Recurrence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and the references there for more discussion of Approximate Graph Coloring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We do not make any attempt at all to optimize the number of colors used in our construction. With our ideas it seems difficult to beat the bounds from the unit distance in the plane. Let us also mention that while it is simple to check that at least <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> colors are necessary for any example answering Katznelson’s Question in the negative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, in this setting of <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-term and longer progressions we do not even know if we can have a counterexample using only <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> colors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Concerning counterexamples with only <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> colors, we also mention here the <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-large versus large conjecture of Brown, Graham, and Landman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. A set <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">large</em> if it is a set of <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-topological recurrence for all <math id="S3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, so that restricted van der Waerden with restricted common difference <math id="S3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> is true for arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions. A set <math id="S3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S3.p12.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">2-large</em> if any 2-coloring of <math id="S3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> has arbitrarily large arithmetic progressions with common difference in <math id="S3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Brown, Graham, and Landman asked if <math id="S3.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-large sets are large. We have constructed the first example of a set that is not large but meets every nil-Bohr set. We conjecture that it is necessary to do so to refute the <math id="S3.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-large versus large conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is not a set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-nilBohr recurrence for some <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-large.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We also ask whether these sets, or some similarly constructed modifications, are <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-large.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are the sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of the kind constructed here <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-large?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Another potential question we can ask is about the polynomial version of Katznelson’s question. What if we consider the Bergelson-Leibman polynomial van der Waerden Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> with restricted differences? We pose one such question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For what sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> does an arbitrary finite coloring of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> always contain a monochromatic <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,x+d,x+d^{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,x+d,x+d^{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in S</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Progress on any of these questions would be interesting and would contribute to our general understanding of recurrence.</p>
</div>
</section>
